1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,241
Why do we drop a giant ball at

2
00:00:02,241 --> 00:00:02,941
New Year?

3
00:00:03,282 --> 00:00:05,010
It started as a signal for ships...

4
00:00:05,548 --> 00:00:07,663
because one wrong clock could send a ship

5
00:00:07,663 --> 00:00:09,110
miles off course.

6
00:00:09,437 --> 00:00:11,190
At sea, sailors worked out where they were

7
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:12,670
with a clock set to home time.

8
00:00:13,228 --> 00:00:15,247
Just one minute wrong could mean fifteen

9
00:00:15,247 --> 00:00:16,113
miles off.

10
00:00:16,412 --> 00:00:18,964
So in 1833, Greenwich put a big ball

11
00:00:18,964 --> 00:00:19,664
on a mast.

12
00:00:20,107 --> 00:00:21,993
Every day at exactly one o'clock, it

13
00:00:21,993 --> 00:00:23,926
dropped, and ships on the river set their

14
00:00:23,926 --> 00:00:24,626
clocks.

15
00:00:24,758 --> 00:00:27,386
In 1907, New York borrowed the idea for

16
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:29,329
New Year's Eve, with a glowing ball that

17
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:30,377
dropped at midnight.

18
00:00:30,685 --> 00:00:32,280
So when the ball falls this New Year...

19
00:00:32,476 --> 00:00:34,437
you're watching an old clock signal for

20
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:34,837
sailors.
